  "query_preview": "..\nUnseasonably warm and dry conditions will continue through\nThursday of this week due to a broad upper level ridge sitting\nover much of the western and central US. The ridge was at its\nstrongest yesterday, but today will still be one of the warmer\ndays of the week. Temperatures for most valleys will be in the\nmid to high 70s, with 80 degree temperatures seen in the basin,\nRitzville, and Lewiston areas. A brief, weak impulse will move\nthrough the area this afternoon, bringing an increase in\nwesterly winds throughout the area. Wind gusts will be 10-15 mph\nacross most places with localized gusts up to 20 mph, so minimal\nimpacts are expected. Temperatures will cool by a couple degrees\nbut still remain unseasonably warm as the ridge strengthens\nagain through Thursday. Low temperatures are not seeing such\nunseasonably warm values due to clear skies and light winds\npromoting radiational cooling. They will remain around normal,\nin the mid 30s for sheltered northern valleys and in the low 40s\nfor Spokane through the basin.\n\nConditions remain this way until Friday, when long term models show \na robust cold front moving through the region. While there is little \nagreement in overall track and timing, ensembles show good\nagreement in temperatures dropping nearly 10 to 12 degrees from\nFriday to Saturday, bringing them back to right around normal.\nForecast highs in the low to mid 60s and lows in mid to high \n30s (lower 30s across sheltered valleys). There is also agreement\non gusty winds accompanying the low, with overall gusts 15 to \n25 mph and gusts 30 to 35 mph possible in the basin and \nWaterville Plateau areas. There are low chances for \nprecipitation, with a 30 percent chance or lower in the \nmountains and even lower elsewhere. /AS",
